int[] array = {10. 20. 30};

Direct Access (ArrayBack):
Stack will reference the memory address of the array, and use base offset calculations within the Heap to retrieve 
the elements within the array from memory contiguously

LinkedList (ListBack): When a List is created, [10] [20] [30] are Nodes, they are not contiguous within memory
Nodes contain a reference address to the next node, linking them together. The final node will have
a NULL address, signifying the end of the List. 
The first Node of the List is called the Head, starting from the head you can navigate through the list using Pointers
Arrays have to allocate memory ahead of time, while Linked List only allocate the space they need, meaning Nodes can be added
without needing to shift memory addresses.
Pointers allow for nodes to be inserted or deleted without shifting all nodes

Drawbacks: There is no Direct Access to a Node, you have to search through the List from start to destination
Worst Case Scenario O(n).
You must maintain your Head Pointer, or else you'll lose access to the list. 
Basic LinkedLists can only read forward, since Nodes have no pointer to the Nodes before them

LinkedList will have multiple Objects on the Stack referring to the same Address, but no matter what, you must maintain
the head address, or the list will be taken up by garbage collection

List uses more memory then Arrays, due to the memory size of the Pointers
